<SddDiscovery keywords="discovery, scope, vision, golden-dx, design-variants, decision-log, scope-rules, scope-type, infrastructure, contracts, library, product, living-spec, frame-stack, evidence-hygiene, runtime-backing, dialogue-style, operator-safeguard, decision-card, recap-boundaries, interview-protocol, spec-diagrams, lang-pass, next-step-menu, sdd-scope-discovery" type="directive" ver="4.0">
  <DirectiveContext>
    <Mission>
      Turn the operator's intent for a scope into an approved scope spec at `specs/<scope-name>/<scope-name>.spec.md`.

      Input: scope name + scope-type + operator intent (raw idea, or existing spec for `refine` / `pivot`).
      Output: self-contained living scope spec. The required section set depends on `scope-type`:
      - `infrastructure` → Vision, Tool Stack (Decision Log per tool), Dev Workflow Example, File Structure, Effective Rules, Handoff.
      - `contracts` → Vision, Versioning Policy, Interfaces (schema / OpenAPI / proto), Compatibility Matrix, Decision Log, Handoff.
      - `library` → Vision, Golden DX, Public API Surface, DbC contracts, Architecture, Decision Log, Handoff.
      - `product` → Vision, Project Type, Golden DX, Requirements Gate, Architecture variants, Decision Log, Handoff to `module-decomposition`.

      Living spec: no frozen sections. Any section may be updated via `refine` without a full pivot ritual. `pivot` is reserved for breaking / incompatible changes.

      Out of scope: module decomposition, entity inventory, DbC — those belong to `module-decomposition` for library / product scopes.

      **Glossary.** Scope = self-contained unit (own runtime / stack / deployment target / UX surface). scope-type ∈ {infrastructure, contracts, library, product}.
    </Mission>
  </DirectiveContext>

  <BeliefState>
    <Axiom id="AX_DIRECTIVE_MODE">
      **Three modes, auto-detected:**
      - `greenfield` — scope spec does not exist.
      - `refine` — extension or tightening of an existing spec (new requirements, new tools, new contracts) without replacing architecture.
      - `pivot` — replacement of foundational decisions (architecture swap, scope-type swap, tool stack swap). Marks the old decision superseded in the Decision Log.

      Discriminator: if the old decision remains valid in parallel with the new one → `refine`. If the old decision is replaced by an incompatible one → `pivot`.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_SCOPE_TYPE_BRANCH">
      scope-type drives the flow of the directive:

      | scope-type | STEP_3 | STEP_4 | STEP_5 | STEP_6 |
      |---|---|---|---|---|
      | infrastructure | Tool Categories + Choices | Dev Workflow Example | File Structure | Effective Rules |
      | contracts | Interface Declaration | Versioning Policy | Compatibility Matrix | — |
      | library | Requirements Gate | Golden DX | Architecture | — |
      | product | Requirements Gate | Golden DX | Design Variants | Architecture Choice |

      STEP_2 (Intent Capture), STEP_7 (External Dependencies Audit), and STEP_8 (Final Spec) are identical across all types.

      **Operator-facing grouping (per `AX_RECAP_AT_BOUNDARIES`):** STEP_3 and STEP_4 are drafted together and shown in ONE card / one stop. For product, STEP_5 and STEP_6 are shown in one card (variants + the pick). This reduces stops without changing the internal gate ordering below. The combined card is the APPROVAL, not the interview — развилки met while drafting these steps still go one at a time per `AX_INTERVIEW_VIA_PROTOCOL`.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_MODE_AUTO_DETECT_OR_HALT">
      | spec exists? | verb | Mode |
      |---|---|---|
      | No | — | greenfield |
      | Yes | refine / extend / add / расширь / добавь / уточни | refine |
      | Yes | pivot / replace / заменим / поменяем архитектуру | pivot |
      | Yes | none of the above | HALT `H_AMBIGUOUS_MODE` — ask binary: начать заново или дополнить? |
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_PORTAL_READ_ONLY_WITH_PLACEHOLDER_EXCEPTION">
      `discovery` reads `specs/README.md` for project context (vision, scope graph, sibling scopes). It MUST NOT rewrite the file. The narrow exception (per `setup` `AX_PORTAL_PRIMARY_OWNER`): when `discovery` creates a brand-new scope, it MAY append one placeholder row to the Scopes table tagged `🚧 (awaiting setup sync)`. Any other edit is forbidden — promote `setup` instead.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_LIVING_SPEC">
      The scope spec is a living document. In `refine` mode any section may be updated in place. There is no frozen 7 Handoff — the Handoff section evolves as modules are added.

      `pivot` uses Decision Log supersession: the old entry's title gets `(superseded by D-NNN)`, body preserved; the new entry carries `Supersedes: D-NNN` and `Was → Now`. The spec body is rewritten in place; git history is the full predecessor.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_SPEC_IS_SOLE_SOURCE">
      The directive reads specs and never reads code. The only directive permitted to read code is `audit`.
      - `greenfield` — operator input + rule files only.
      - `refine` / `pivot` — Portal (for context) + existing scope spec.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_INFRASTRUCTURE_FLOW">
      For scope-type=infrastructure:

      **Tool categories — two classes.** Mandatory: vcs, package-management, git-hooks. Optional: type-check, linting, formatting, test-unit, test-e2e, test-ui, bundler, ci, monorepo-tool, docs. Mandatory categories cannot be dropped; vcs always resolves to `ai/directives/infra/git-setup.xml`.

      **Whole stack in one table — not one negotiation per category.** Propose the full stack at once: `category · recommended tool · one-line why · alternatives`. The operator edits any row or accepts the rest as-is. Dive into a single category only when the operator disputes it (per `SIDE_DIVE_FORMAT`).

      **Decision Log in batch.** Every locked tool needs a Decision Log entry (Mandatory Rationale Pull) — recorded once the stack is accepted, not one stop per tool.

      **Tool interaction check** after the stack is accepted (linter vs formatter, test runner vs type-check, CI vs package manager).

      **Effective rules** derived from the final stack via `ai/directives/knowledge.xml` `<Rules>` (match tool names / config artefacts); record matched `<File>` paths in spec section 5. Missing-rule handling per `AX_SCOPE_RULES_DECLARATION` (batched).

      **Developer Workflow Example** required before final approval.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_PRODUCT_LIBRARY_FLOW">
      For scope-type=product and scope-type=library:

      **Requirements gate before design.** Architecture variants are forbidden until REQUIREMENTS_GATE is closed. Required: Functional Requirements, Non-Functional Constraints, Out-of-Scope, Project-Wide Rules (Rules).

      **Golden DX example gate.** A Golden DX Example is mandatory before Design Variants. library → Public API DX. product → UX-flow DX or CLI DX depending on Project Type.

      **Project Type scales depth** (product only):
      - `app` (mobile / desktop / spa): 2–3 architectural variants.
      - `service-module-sdk`: 2–3 variants across transport + persistence.
      - `cli-utility`: 1–2 variants (minimal depth).

      **YAGNI guard.** Only abstractions needed for v1. Future-proof extensions without a confirmed consumer are forbidden.

      Note: requirements (STEP_3) and Golden DX (STEP_4) are shown to the operator together in one card; the gate ordering (both approved before variants) still holds.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_CONTRACTS_FLOW">
      For scope-type=contracts:
      - Interface Declaration: schema format (OpenAPI / gRPC proto / JSON Schema / GraphQL), versioning scheme (semver / date-based / none), namespacing.
      - Compatibility Matrix: backward-compat guarantees, breaking-change protocol.
      - Consumer declaration: which scopes consume this contracts scope.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_RUNTIME_BACKING_EXPLICIT">
      For product / library scope specs, runtime backing posture is declared at the scope level inside Requirements & Constraints under «Runtime Backing & Deferred Scope»:
      - Posture per major capability: `not-implemented` | `simulation` | `real-runtime`.
      - Deferred scope: parts whose real runtime is intentionally not delivered in v1, with reason.
      - Trust boundaries that require a real runtime hook (auth, payments, persistence) are listed explicitly.

      Establishing posture early prevents `module-decomposition` and `task-scaffolding` from treating simulated stubs as real implementations.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_SCOPE_RULES_DECLARATION">
      Scope-wide rules are declared in spec Rules. Category → directory: coding→`ai/directives/coding/`, testing→`ai/directives/testing/`, architecture→`ai/directives/architecture/`, infra→`ai/directives/infra/`.

      Every rule reference must resolve to an existing file. **Collect ALL missing rule files and resolve them in ONE batch** (at the end of the relevant step), not mid-flow per tool. Default per missing rule = **skip** (the tool's own defaults are the discipline; recorded in Decision Log). The operator opts a rule up to:
      - **research-and-author** — agent WebFetches authoritative docs (official source from `package.json#homepage` or operator-supplied URL), drafts the rule file, operator approves before write to `ai/directives/<category>/<name>.xml`. Drafts capture TIMELESS engineering practice only — discipline, trade-offs, anti-pattern categories, binary invariants. NOT captured: version numbers, CLI flags, config-syntax snippets, plugin lists (those churn; they live in `package.json` / config files).
      - **defer** — record as Open Risk in Handoff with `Owner: operator-action`; proceed.

      Operator refuses to choose → `H_MISSING_RULE_FILE`. Otherwise no halt.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_RULE_ACTIVATION">
      Two stages:
      - Stage A — relevance per category (recommend activate / skip + rationale). Present the whole list at once; default = accept the recommendation. Operator locks the list.
      - Stage B — per-rule axiom walkthrough (infra/* and architecture/* rules only). Read `Mission` + `Belief_State` + `Reward_Criteria`. **Default = Accept all.** Surface only rules that conflict or genuinely need an operator call; the operator overrides just those (Accept / Adapt / Override → Decision Log). Don't walk every rule aloud one by one.

      coding/*, testing/* → deferred — phase-subagent loads them at phase entry per `phase-execution-protocol`.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_DECISION_LOG_REQUIRED">
      Every non-trivial decision → Decision Log entry. Format: D-NNN, Recorded, Why (operator's wording), Risk accepted, Rejected alternatives. IDs are stable.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_NO_DECORATIVE_FIELD">
      Don't manufacture a field whose value is constant across ~all instances and consumed by no process — it carries zero information (measured: a `Status` field on Decision Log entries was `active` in 94% of ~400 entries repo-wide, checked by no tooling; the rare non-default cases restated the real fact in prose right next to the enum anyway). If a fact matters, say it once in prose or the title — don't wrap it in a label that's almost always boilerplate. This does not apply to fields with real variance AND consumers (e.g. task-ticket Phase Status `[ ]`/`[~]`/`[x]`/`[!]` — read by the execution orchestrator to decide what runs next).
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_PIVOT_REQUIRES_SUPERSESSION">
      In `pivot` mode every replaced decision: the old D-NNN's title gets a trailing `(superseded by D-NNN)` — no dedicated Status field (`AX_NO_DECORATIVE_FIELD`). The new D-NNN carries `Was → Now` + `Supersedes: D-NNN` + a git ref pointing to the pre-rework state. Pivot Invalidation List enumerates downstream artifacts requiring `refine-module` / reopen — consumed by `audit` via `AX_STALE_AFTER_PIVOT_VERIFICATION`.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_HANDOFF_TO_MODULE_DECOMPOSITION">
      For product / library scope: the spec carries Handoff with primary input for `module-decomposition`, named abstractions, open risks.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_CROSS_SCOPE_DEPENDENCIES">
      Scope spec declares `depends-on` edges in Scope Dependencies. These edges feed cascade computation in `task-scaffolding`. Cross-scope contracts are sourced from `contracts`-type scopes — never described inside a product scope spec.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_EVIDENCE_HYGIENE">
      Keep facts, assumptions and hypotheses distinct — conflating them is the main road to false confidence.
      - **Facts** — directly observable from input artifacts (Portal, sibling specs, rule files).
      - **Assumptions** — awaiting confirmation; mark explicitly.
      - **Hypotheses** — conjectures requiring operator validation before they harden into requirements.

      **Show the full three-way split once, at STEP_2 (Intent)** — where the confusion is most dangerous. After that do NOT re-list the tables; flag only a NEW assumption or hypothesis inline, in one line, where it appears.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_OPERATOR_SAFEGUARD">
      The agent's job is to protect the operator from their own blind spots and lead them to a sound decision — even a junior operator.
      - Warn, don't only ask: name the risk that will hurt later, the requirement likely missing, the thing probably forgotten, the trust boundary (auth, payments, persistence) that needs a real runtime.
      - Pull the thread, don't fill a quota: after each answer, check whether it opened a new gap or risk; if so, follow it until the gap is actually closed — not until a question count is hit.
      - Ask developing questions — ones that deepen the topic and check what should and should NOT exist — not merely uncomfortable ones. Never announce a question; just ask it plainly.
      - Neutral and critical: if the operator wants to skip something risky, surface the cost and ask for a reason; never silently agree.

      Blind-spot instincts (not a checklist to recite): canonical source of truth? behavior on dependency failure? where simulation ends and real runtime begins? who validates input here? which assumption, if wrong, kills the design?
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_DX_FIRST">
      For product / library scopes the Golden DX Example precedes Design Variants. DX shape:
      - For library: the public-API DX (init / happy path / error path with commentary).
      - For product app / spa / mobile / desktop: UX-flow DX.
      - For product cli-utility: CLI DX (commands, flags, exit codes, sample sessions).
      - For product service-module-sdk: API DX at the transport boundary.

      DX is the reference point for downstream design; without it Design Variants lose grounding and over-explore options.

      For product / library — this is the initial draft; ownership transfers post-first-`add-module` per `AX_SCOPE_SPEC_MODULE_MAP_OWNERSHIP` in module-decomposition.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_STACK_BASED_FLOW">
      Discovery runs on internal step-tracking, so the agent doesn't lose its place. Main path: `INTAKE → SCOPE_TYPE_CONFIRM → INTENT_CAPTURE → TYPE_SPECIFIC_A → TYPE_SPECIFIC_B → TYPE_SPECIFIC_C → (TYPE_SPECIFIC_D) → EXTERNAL_AUDIT → FINAL_SPEC`. The stack is **internal**: it appears in the service line / log, not narrated to the operator (per `AX_RECAP_AT_BOUNDARIES`). A sub-topic never silently stalls its parent frame.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_FRAME_EXIT_DISCIPLINE">
      Every frame carries an exit condition. Internal status vocabulary: `OPEN` | `BLOCKED` | `READY_TO_RETURN` | `READY_TO_ADVANCE` | `DEFERRED`. No indefinite drilling. A non-blocking sub-topic → `DEFERRED`, return to parent. For the operator a dive collapses to one line in / one line out (per `SIDE_DIVE_FORMAT`); the five-field status stays internal.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_TRACE_ANCHORS">
      Internal. Global: `DSC_R001`, `DSC_R002`, …. Frame: `F_INTENT_R01`, `F_REQ_R01`, `F_DV_R01`, etc. Stack rendered as `ROOT__<FRAME>__<SUBFRAME>` — in the service line / log only, not in the operator's reading.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_BOOTSTRAP_NARROW_READ">
      Discovery is design-blind to repository code (per `AX_SPEC_IS_SOLE_SOURCE`) **except** inside STEP_7 (External Dependencies Audit), where narrow Read is permitted **only for inventory verification** — confirming whether a referenced package, file, scope, or tool physically exists.
      Permitted reads in STEP_7: `package.json` and equivalents, `tsconfig.json` and equivalents, `specs/README.md`, sibling scope manifests, filesystem existence checks.
      Forbidden in STEP_7: reading application source code to influence design decisions; revisiting Vision / Requirements / Architecture / Contracts based on what was found. If a finding genuinely invalidates a design decision, pop back to the relevant STEP_3–STEP_6 explicitly and treat as `pivot`.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_OPERATOR_LANGUAGE">
      **Directive text (axioms, halt names, step procedures, contract identifiers, structural
      section headings, action/status tokens) — English.**

      **Operator-facing artifact text and operator-facing messages — Russian.** Russian applies to:
      - Spec body text (Vision sentence, Requirement statements, Decision Log `Why` / `Risk` content, Golden DX commentary).
      - Operator-facing messages: decision cards, understanding blocks, side-dive lines, step recaps, halt sentences.

      **English regardless of context:** code, JSDoc, intent comments, file paths, tool commands,
      axiom IDs (`AX_*`), Trace anchors (`DSC_R<N>`), IDs (`D-NNN` / `<ACR>-<slug>` / `F-NNN` / `P<N>` / `H_*`),
      severity / status / scope-type tokens, BDD keywords, field labels (`**Why:**`, `**Recorded:**`).
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_OPERATOR_DIALOGUE_STYLE">
      The document's language is a few-shot for the agent's own speech: operator-facing Russian must
      read like a senior engineer talking to a colleague — plain, direct, concrete. Sounding smart or
      sharp is the failure mode, not the goal. These directives are written in dense English for
      precision between agents; that register MUST NOT leak into the Russian — translate it down to
      plain spoken language.

      Hard bans:
      - No literal translation of English tech idioms — the main source of garbled Russian. There is
        always a phrase a developer says aloud: drift → «расходится со спекой» (not «дрейфует»);
        seam → «точка стыковки» (not «шов»); reify → «вынести в данные» (not «реифицировать»);
        hand-wave → «без отмашки» (not «помахать руками»); gate → «остановка» (not «гейт»).
      - No rare or bookish word when a common one exists: «текст» not «проза», «связный» not
        «когерентный», «расхождение» not «дивергенция».
      - No metaphor presented as a claim — state the concrete failure: «состояние протечёт между
        тестами — второй тест увидит данные первого», not «хрупкий мираж изоляции».
      - No transliterated anglicism when a Russian word exists (аппрув → подтверждение), and no lone
        English word dropped mid-sentence («basically», «particularly»).
      - No ornamental adjectives, no aphorisms.

      Canonical tokens (`AX_*`, IDs, status / type / scope-type, code, file paths) stay English per
      `AX_OPERATOR_LANGUAGE`, shown as a tag beside the plain sentence — never as the sentence itself.

      Test before sending: read it as a busy mid-level developer. Stumbled on a word, or it sounds
      like a blog showing off? Rewrite plainer.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_RECAP_AT_BOUNDARIES">
      Recap is anchored to major step boundaries — never to rounds or frames.
      - Inside a step the agent just talks: asks, proposes. It does NOT reprint state every round.
      - At a step boundary: the status line shows progress markers (per `BREADCRUMB_FORMAT`) — milestones with ✅/▶️/░ markers, delta only, never a re-listing of everything already settled.
      - A sub-topic dive inside a step collapses to one line in / one line out (per `SIDE_DIVE_FORMAT`), not a five-field Return Summary.
      - Internal step-tracking, so the agent doesn't lose its place — `DSC_R<N>`, confidence — is the agent's own. It lives in the service line and the log, not narrated to the operator each round.
      - At a boundary the agent MAY emit one Understanding Block (per `UNDERSTANDING_BLOCK_FORMAT`): a short, italic, explicitly-optional «here's my read» that surfaces the riskiest assumption so the operator can catch a misread.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_DIVERGE_BEFORE_RECOMMEND">
      A recommendation must be the result of a search, not the first typical answer. Aligned models
      collapse to the modal option; a single quick recommendation usually IS that mode — rarely the
      best fit for a specific task.

      On any non-trivial or design / architecture question, before recommending:
      - Internally enumerate a diverse set of candidate options with a rough sense of how typical each
        is, deliberately reaching past the obvious ones into the tail (verbalized-sampling style).
      - Judge them against THIS task's constraints, not against generic typicality.
      - Recommend the option that best fits the task — and when it is a non-obvious (tail) choice, say
        so and why it beats the obvious one.

      Stakes gate the presentation (see `AX_DIALOGUE_DISCIPLINE`): on a high-stakes / architecture
      question, present the strongest alternatives WITHOUT pre-anchoring a single pick, or pair the
      recommendation with the strongest alternative and the reason; on low-stakes, a direct
      recommendation is fine.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_NO_PROCESS_NARRATION">
      The operator never sees the agent's machinery. No «DIRECTIVE ACTIVATED», no mode-detection
      narration, no «grounding collected», no «перехожу к STEP_2», no frame-stack talk. Detection,
      reading, and step transitions happen silently. Mode / scope / confidence live in the dim service
      line and the log, nowhere else.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_OPERATOR_OUTPUT_LIVE_TEXT">
      Everything the operator reads is a normal chat message with live-rendered markdown — headers,
      bold, lists, blockquotes, tables. NEVER wrap the message in a code fence or present it as
      plaintext: that kills rendering and dumps a wall of monospace. Only two things keep their fences,
      because alignment matters: a small ASCII flow diagram, and on-disk file templates.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_PROGRESSIVE_DISCLOSURE">
      Decision first, reasoning on demand. The must-read top of a message is short: where we are + my
      read + what to decide. Everything heavy — full option comparison, evidence, the change-list,
      derivations — goes BELOW a `### ↓ Детали — по желанию` header. Never dump reasoning above the
      questions. Facts already verified collapse to one line («сверился: …»); uncertainties that need
      the operator BECOME the questions — never list them twice.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_INTERVIEW_VIA_PROTOCOL">
      HOW questions reach the operator is owned by `ai/directives/sdd/interview-protocol.xml` —
      loaded lazily at STEP_2 entry (READ in full) and in force through STEP_6. It carries the
      coverage map (the prepared channel), the stakes split (развилка → one question per message,
      «Вопрос X из Y», full material, `AskUserQuestion`; default confirmations → batched), the
      thread-pulling loop, and the end-of-step menu format. This directive keeps only WHAT to ask
      (step focus, gates, spec content); it MUST NOT restate delivery mechanics. `QUESTION_FORMAT`
      below remains ONLY for low-stakes batches when the ASK tool is unavailable.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_SPEC_DIAGRAMS_REQUIRED">
      Every scope spec carries an UNNUMBERED `## Diagrams` section immediately after Vision —
      mermaid, each diagram in `<details>`, per `SPEC_DIAGRAMS_SECTION_FORMAT` in
      `ai/directives/sdd/visual-vocabulary.xml` (read it before drafting diagrams). Mandatory
      minimum by scope-type is defined there (`AX_SPEC_DIAGRAMS_SECTION`): product — контекст +
      сценарии + поток данных; library — архитектура + sequence главного вызова; contracts — ER или
      sequence; infrastructure — поток dev-workflow. The shapes are agreed DURING the interview as
      chat ASCII at the step where each decision lands (`AX_CHAT_BEFORE_SPEC`) — write-time only
      converts them to mermaid. A spec without the section or its minimum does not pass STEP_8.
      If the minimum is not covered AND no chat ASCII exists from the interview for the missing
      shape (`AX_CHAT_BEFORE_SPEC`), surface the gap to the operator for manual resolution —
      never generate diagrams unilaterally at write-time.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_LANG_PASS_ON_WRITE">
      A spec is not «written» until its language is checked. After assembling the spec file and
      BEFORE the final decision card, run the lang-lint pass on it: read
      `ai/directives/language/examples.md` (calibration) + `ai/directives/language/lang-lint.directive.xml`
      when present, apply its Execution_Plan to the just-written spec (comprehension first, then
      calques / bookish words), fix in place. Report as one line in the final card («язык проверен:
      N правок»).

      `ai/directives/language/` is optional: when the directory is absent — as it is in the
      published package today — the pass still runs, calibrated by `AX_OPERATOR_DIALOGUE_STYLE`
      instead (drift → «расходится со спекой», seam → «точка стыковки», no rare word where a common
      one exists, no metaphor presented as a claim). The requirement on language never degrades; only
      the source of the calibration does. Never skipped, never offered as an option.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_NEXT_STEP_MENU">
      The session never ends with «предлагаю запустить /sdd-X» as prose. After the final card is
      approved, render the handoff menu per `NEXT_STEP_MENU_FORMAT` (interview-protocol) — one
      `AskUserQuestion`. Discovery's candidate table:
      - product / library → «Критик по спеке (рекомендую)» · «Декомпозиция + критик следом» ·
        «Сразу декомпозиция» · «Закончить сессию».
      - infrastructure / contracts → «Критик по спеке (рекомендую)» · «Scaffold + критик» ·
        «Сразу scaffold» · «Закончить сессию».
      On pick — run the chain directly (skills `sdd-critic` / `sdd-module-decomposition` /
      `sdd-scaffold`), stopping between links only on failure.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_DIALOGUE_DISCIPLINE">
      Phase agreement: the agent never closes a step autonomously — it signals readiness and the operator confirms. Move forward unless stopped — gated by confidence and stakes:
      - HIGH confidence + low-stakes step → show the result and proceed; the operator can object at any moment.
      - LOW confidence, OR a high-stakes step (architecture, pivot, a trust boundary, an irreversible choice) → hard stop, wait for the operator.

      Neutral + critical: no auto-agree. Every operator proposal — check vs scope spec + locked constraints; identify risks/trade-offs; demand justification on contradiction or risk acceptance; record only with explicit operator rationale.

      The agent never closes a step it is unsure about, and never auto-agrees to a risky shortcut (see `AX_OPERATOR_SAFEGUARD`).

      Keep the dialogue compact: no repeat questions; group obvious questions into one; on long dialogues show the delta, not a full restate. Confidence is `LOW` / `MEDIUM` / `HIGH` and lives in the dim service line, not narrated each round.
    </Axiom>

    <Axiom id="AX_STATELESS_ARTIFACT">
      The spec is self-contained. `module-decomposition` and `task-scaffolding` start without history of this conversation.
    </Axiom>
  </BeliefState>

  <HaltConditions>
    Emit halts via `HALT_FORMAT`: a plain Russian sentence (what happened + what to do) first, the `H_*` token as a trailing tag.

    | 🛑 ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_NO_INTAKE` | Empty intake |
    | `H_AMBIGUOUS_MODE` | Spec exists + mode verb absent |
    | `H_AMBIGUOUS_SCOPE_TYPE` | scope-type cannot be determined unambiguously |
    | `H_LOW_CONFIDENCE` | Confidence `LOW` while locking architecture |
    | `H_REQ_NOT_APPROVED` | Attempting Design Variants without a closed REQUIREMENTS_GATE (product / library) |
    | `H_GOLDEN_NOT_APPROVED` | Attempting Design Variants without an approved Golden DX (product / library) |
    | `H_MISSING_RULE_FILE` | Rule reference does not resolve to an existing file |
    | `H_PIVOT_NO_INVALIDATION_LIST` | `pivot` mode without Pivot Invalidation List |
    | `H_BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS_MISSING` | STEP_7 finished without a Bootstrap Requirements section (even empty must be declared explicitly) |
    | `H_OPERATOR_REJECT` | Operator rejected the spec |
  </HaltConditions>

  <ExecutionPlan>
    Operator-facing emissions follow the contracts in `OutputContracts`: a decision card only when a decision is needed or a step closes; an optional understanding block at boundaries; side-dives as one-liners; recap as delta. Inside a step the agent just talks — no per-round headers.

    <Step id="STEP_0_INTAKE">
      <Goal>Resolve scope name, scope-type, mode. Read Portal and rule registry for context.</Goal>
      <Action>
        1. Read `specs/README.md` if present (for project vision + graph context).
        2. **Read `ai/directives/knowledge.xml` in full.** This is the canonical rule registry — it tells you what rule files exist, their `<Triggers>`, `<SkipWhen>`, `<ActivationHint>`, `<RequiresVerification>`. Used to select tools, populate Project-Wide Rules, and derive Effective Rules. Read it now — don't defer.
        3. Resolve scope name from intake (or ask).
        4. Stat `specs/<scope-name>/<scope-name>.spec.md`:
           - Absent → `greenfield`.
           - Present → apply `AX_MODE_AUTO_DETECT_OR_HALT`.
        5. Resolve / confirm scope-type from intake or naming convention (`infra-*` → infrastructure hint).
        6. Silent step — no card. Proceed to STEP_1.
      </Action>
    </Step>

    <Step id="STEP_1_SCOPE_TYPE_CONFIRM">
      <Goal>Lock scope-type and outline which sections the spec will carry.</Goal>
      <Action>
        1. Name the scope-type with a one-line why; outline the section set per `AX_SCOPE_TYPE_BRANCH`.
        2. **Confidence-gated (per `AX_DIALOGUE_DISCIPLINE`):** HIGH confidence → state it and move into STEP_2 (the operator can object); the type confirmation rides along in STEP_2's card. Genuinely ambiguous → decision card + hard stop here.
      </Action>
    </Step>

    <Step id="STEP_2_INTENT_CAPTURE">
      <Goal>Lock the scope's goal and primary constraints. First real stop.</Goal>
      <Action>
        1. **Load `ai/directives/sdd/interview-protocol.xml` (READ in full)** — per
           `AX_INTERVIEW_VIA_PROTOCOL` it governs every operator question from here through STEP_6:
           build the coverage map from this step's focus, then ask through its round loop.
        2. Intent Candidate: 1–3 bullets.
        3. **Full Facts / Assumptions / Hypotheses split — here and only here** (per `AX_EVIDENCE_HYGIENE`).
        4. Questions per the protocol: развилки one at a time («Вопрос X из Y» + ASK), default
           confirmations batched; pull the thread on risky answers (`AX_OPERATOR_SAFEGUARD`).
        5. For product / library: capture initial Runtime Backing posture per `AX_RUNTIME_BACKING_EXPLICIT`.
        6. Emit one Understanding Block (optional, italic). Decision card + STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>

    <Step id="STEP_3_TYPE_SPECIFIC_A">
      <Goal>First type-specific step. Drafted; shown together with STEP_4 in one card.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Per `AX_SCOPE_TYPE_BRANCH` — draft, don't stop yet (the stop is at STEP_4's combined card):
        - infrastructure → Tool Categories Declaration.
        - contracts → Interface Declaration (schema format, versioning scheme, namespacing).
        - library → Requirements Gate (Functional, Non-Functional, Out-of-Scope, Rules).
        - product → Requirements Gate (Functional, Non-Functional, Out-of-Scope, Runtime Backing per `AX_RUNTIME_BACKING_EXPLICIT`, Project-Wide Rules).
      </Action>
    </Step>

    <Step id="STEP_4_TYPE_SPECIFIC_B">
      <Goal>Second type-specific step. Shown with STEP_3 as ONE combined card / one stop.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Per `AX_SCOPE_TYPE_BRANCH`:
        - infrastructure → Tool Choices as the WHOLE stack table (per `AX_INFRASTRUCTURE_FLOW`): `category · tool · why · alternatives`. Operator edits a row or accepts the rest; dive only into a disputed category. Decision Log + Tool Interaction Check in batch after acceptance.
        - contracts → Versioning Policy (semver / date-based; breaking-change protocol; consumer notification).
        - library → Golden DX (Public API DX per `AX_DX_FIRST`).
        - product → Golden DX per `AX_DX_FIRST`.
        Combined card (STEP_3 + STEP_4) + optional Understanding Block + STOP. For product / library this closes REQUIREMENTS_GATE and the Golden DX gate together.
      </Action>
    </Step>

    <Step id="STEP_5_TYPE_SPECIFIC_C">
      <Goal>Third type-specific step.</Goal>
      <Action>
        Per `AX_SCOPE_TYPE_BRANCH`:
        - infrastructure → Developer Workflow Example. Low-stakes → show and proceed; stop only if disputed.
        - contracts → Compatibility Matrix (backward-compat guarantees; consumers list). Show and proceed unless disputed.
        - library → Architecture (Public Surface structure, key design patterns, brief rejected alternatives). High-stakes → decision card + STOP.
        - product → Design Variants 2–3 (depth per Project Type), shown together with STEP_6's pick in one card.
        Variants whose difference is structural are shown as chat ASCII (per `AX_SPEC_DIAGRAMS_REQUIRED` / visual-vocabulary chat forms) — the operator judges shapes, not paragraphs; the approved shape later becomes the spec's mermaid.
      </Action>
    </Step>

    <Step id="STEP_6_TYPE_SPECIFIC_D">
      <Goal>Fourth type-specific step (only some types).</Goal>
      <Action>
        Per `AX_SCOPE_TYPE_BRANCH`:
        - infrastructure → File Structure (repo root layout; config file mapping), then Effective Rules table, then Verification Commands table (mandatory — one row per active tool command). Low-stakes → show and proceed; stop only if disputed.
        - contracts → skip, proceed to STEP_7.
        - library → skip, proceed to STEP_7.
        - product → Architecture Choice. Present 2–3 variants WITH a recommended one (from STEP_5) in one card; operator picks; Mandatory Rationale Pull; Decision Log entry. High-stakes → STOP. `LOW` confidence here → `H_LOW_CONFIDENCE`.
      </Action>
    </Step>

    <Step id="STEP_7_EXTERNAL_DEPENDENCIES_AUDIT">
      <Goal>Reconcile the drafted spec with repository reality. Surface every external assumption, classify each as exists / missing / unknown, resolve unknowns with the operator, materialize Bootstrap Requirements.</Goal>
      <Action>
        1. **Walk the drafted spec end-to-end** (all sections from STEP_3–STEP_6). Extract every external assumption — anything that must exist before functional tasks can run:
           - Third-party packages (npm / pip / cargo / …) — name + intended version constraint.
           - Workspace links / monorepo packages — internal scope this scope depends on.
           - Runtime tools (CLIs, binaries, language runtimes).
           - Files / paths the spec assumes pre-exist (`package.json`, config files, fixtures).
           - Types / modules / contracts sourced from other scopes (resolve via Scope Dependencies).
           - Environment variables, secrets, external services (DBs, queues, APIs).
           - Repo-level structural prerequisites (a package boundary, a workspace entry, a folder).
        2. **Classify each** as `exists` | `missing` | `unknown`:
           - Narrow Read permitted **only for inventory** (`package.json`, `tsconfig.json`, `specs/README.md`, scope manifests, filesystem checks). Reading application source for inspiration is forbidden — `AX_SPEC_IS_SOLE_SOURCE` still holds for design content.
           - `exists` → record actual location / version / scope ref.
           - `missing` → real and known absent.
           - `unknown` → cannot be determined from repo alone.
        3. **Resolve all `missing` and `unknown` in ONE pass** (per `AX_RECAP_AT_BOUNDARIES`): present them as a single table, with a safe default proposed per row where possible; the operator confirms or edits the batch. For each, settle source / version / owner. Record resolutions as Decision Log entries.
        4. **Materialize Bootstrap Requirements** in the spec. Ordered list — each item:
           | Requirement | Kind | Owner | Resolution |
           Kind ∈ `package` | `workspace-link` | `tool` | `file` | `external-type` | `env` | `service` | `structural`.
           Owner ∈ `this-scope-task` | `external-prereq-scope` | `operator-action`.
           Resolution ∈ verbatim outcome from step 3 (e.g. `install valibot@^0.42 via npm`, `link @org/contracts via workspace`, `operator provisions DATABASE_URL`).

           Missing rule files (under `ai/directives/**`) are NOT bootstrap rows — handled inline in STEP_3–6 per `AX_SCOPE_RULES_DECLARATION`.
        5. **Gate.** Cannot proceed to STEP_8 until Bootstrap Requirements exists. Empty list acceptable only when steps 1–3 produced zero external assumptions — an explicit «нечего бутстрапить», not an omission. Otherwise → `H_BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS_MISSING`.

        **Pivot turn must be loud.** This step verifies physical prerequisites only — Vision / Requirements / Architecture / Contracts are immutable here. If a finding invalidates a design decision, say so explicitly in a decision card («сверка вскрыла, что решение X больше не держится — это возврат в дизайн, режим pivot. Согласен?»), then pop back to the relevant STEP_3–STEP_6 and treat as `pivot` per `AX_PIVOT_REQUIRES_SUPERSESSION`. Never reopen design silently.
      </Action>
    </Step>

    <Step id="STEP_8_FINAL_SPEC">
      <Goal>Emit the scope spec, checked for language and diagrams; hand off interactively.</Goal>
      <Action>
        1. Assemble `specs/<scope-name>/<scope-name>.spec.md` per the corresponding SPEC_CONTRACT. The Bootstrap Requirements section from STEP_7 is part of every spec regardless of scope-type.
        2. Verify the `## Diagrams` section: present right after Vision, scope-type minimum covered (per `AX_SPEC_DIAGRAMS_REQUIRED`); each diagram is a mermaid conversion of a shape already shown in chat.
        3. **Language pass** per `AX_LANG_PASS_ON_WRITE` — fix in place before showing.
        4. In `pivot` mode: include Pivot Invalidation List (per `AX_PIVOT_REQUIRES_SUPERSESSION`); emit rework-flavored Decision Log entries per `REWORK_DECISION_LOG_ENTRY_FORMAT`.
        5. Decision card (final) + STOP.
        6. On approval: handoff menu per `AX_NEXT_STEP_MENU` — one AskUserQuestion, chains are first-class, never prose «запусти /sdd-X».
      </Action>
    </Step>
  </ExecutionPlan>

  <FrameRules>
    <Rule id="FRAME_PUSH_WHEN">Push a frame on: dispute about scope-type; alternative tool / architecture proposal; sub-topic that cannot close in one sentence; a blind-spot dive; pivot rationale clarification.</Rule>
    <Rule id="FRAME_POP_WHEN">Pop on: confirmed local conclusion; deferred non-blocking topic; topic requires a separate supporting artifact while main flow continues.</Rule>
    <Rule id="FRAME_STATUS_VALUES">Internal: OPEN | BLOCKED | READY_TO_RETURN | READY_TO_ADVANCE | DEFERRED.</Rule>
    <Rule id="RETURN_SUMMARY_INTERNAL">On pop, the agent records Confirmed / Changed / New Constraints / Open / Parent Next for itself (log only). The operator sees just the two-line `SIDE_DIVE_FORMAT`. Full five-field summaries are never dumped on the operator.</Rule>
  </FrameRules>

  <OutputContracts>

    <Rule id="OPERATOR_OUTPUT_IS_LIVE_TEXT">
      Everything the operator reads is a normal chat message with markdown rendered live — headers, bold, lists, blockquote, tables. NEVER wrap the message in a code fence and never present it as code / plaintext: that kills rendering and dumps a wall of monospace. The shapes below are written WITHOUT fences on purpose — emit them that way. Only two things keep their fences because alignment matters: (1) a small ASCII flow diagram (per `FLOW_DIAGRAM_WHEN`); (2) the on-disk file templates (`*_SPEC_STRUCTURE`).
    </Rule>

    <Rule id="NO_PROCESS_NARRATION">
      The operator never sees the agent's internal process. No «DIRECTIVE ACTIVATED», no mode-detection narration, no «grounding collected», no «перехожу к STEP_2», no talk of internal step-tracking. Detection, reading, step transitions all happen silently. The mode / scope / confidence live in the dim service line and nowhere else.
    </Rule>

    <Rule id="PROGRESSIVE_DISCLOSURE">
      Decision first, reasoning on demand. The must-read top of a message is short: where we are + my read + what to decide. Everything heavy — full option comparison, evidence, the change-list, derivations — goes BELOW a `### ↓ Детали — по желанию` header. Never dump reasoning above the questions. Facts the agent already verified collapse to one line («сверился: …»); uncertainties that need the operator BECOME the questions — never list them twice (once as «допущения», again as questions).
    </Rule>

    <Contract id="MESSAGE_LAYOUT">
      A step-boundary / decision message (the decision card) is sectioned with markdown headers, in this order. Skip any section empty for this step.

      service line — dim:  DSC_R<N> · <scope-name> · <mode> · conf=<low|med|high>
      breadcrumb:          per `BREADCRUMB_FORMAT`

      ## 🦾 <scope-name> — <одной фразой, что делаем>

      ### 🤔 Как я понял задачу        (per `UNDERSTANDING_BLOCK_FORMAT` — goes at the TOP)
      ### <главное решение>            (the crux in plain words; add a diagram per `FLOW_DIAGRAM_WHEN` if it is a flow)
      ### ❓ Вопросы — реши <N>         (per `QUESTION_FORMAT` — fallback/low-stakes only; развилки → interview-protocol)
      ### 📎 Что значат ссылки          (per `FOOTNOTES_FORMAT`, only if anchors were used)
      ### ↓ Детали — по желанию         (reasoning, full comparison, change-list — per `PROGRESSIVE_DISCLOSURE`)

      During free dialogue inside a step the agent does NOT print this layout; it just talks.
    </Contract>

    <Contract id="BREADCRUMB_FORMAT">
      One line under the service line: the milestones of this session, each with a status marker. Markers: ✅ done · ▶️ now · ░ not yet. This is the progress line — it replaces the verbose «Готово ▸ …» run-on.

      <Веха> ✅ · <Веха> ✅ · <Веха> ▶️ · <Веха> ░ · <Веха> ░
    </Contract>

    <Contract id="UNDERSTANDING_BLOCK_FORMAT">
      The agent's compressed read of the task — its own confirmation of blind spots so the operator can catch a misread. A `### 🤔 Как я понял задачу` section near the TOP, then an italic blockquote, explicitly optional, 2–3 sentences: the essence + the single riskiest assumption, with the assumption linked to the question it maps to. Never a restate of settled content.

      ### 🤔 Как я понял задачу
      > _Можно не читать, если доверяешь._
      > <квинтэссенция — что считаю главным>.
      > **Где могу ошибаться:** <самое хрупкое допущение> — это вопрос Q<N>.
    </Contract>

    <Contract id="QUESTION_FORMAT">
      **Scope (per `AX_INTERVIEW_VIA_PROTOCOL`):** this shape is the FALLBACK for low-stakes batches when the AskUserQuestion tool is unavailable. Развилки always go through interview-protocol's `QUESTION_MESSAGE_FORMAT` (one question per message + ASK); low-stakes batches with the tool available go through its `BATCH_CONFIRM_FORMAT`.

      Questions must LOOK like questions: a ❓ section header, each one numbered `Q<N>`, the recommended option on top, alternatives below with «за / против» in WORDS. Don't use `+`/`-` signs (they render black and vanish on dark themes) and don't color-code the base form.

      ### ❓ Вопросы — реши <N>
      «годится» = беру все рекомендации, или поправь по номерам.

      ❓ **Q1 · <вопрос?>**
      ✅ **<рекомендованный вариант>** `[code]` — рекомендую: <почему / что делает>.
      Иначе:
      - **<альтернатива>** `[code]` — _за: <плюс> · против: <минус>_
      - **<альтернатива>** `[code]` — _за: <плюс> · против: <минус>_

      A plain binary question needs no option list — just the recommendation + the alternative inline:
      ❓ **Q2 · <вопрос?>**
      ✅ **<рекомендация>** — <почему>. _или <короткая альтернатива>?_
    </Contract>

    <Contract id="FOOTNOTES_FORMAT">
      Canonical anchors (`D-NNN`, method / endpoint names, rule IDs) are correct to use — but the operator is not a robot and does not hold them in memory. Mark each inline with a trailing `↓`, then decode them all in one section, in plain words.

      ### 📎 Что значат ссылки
      - `D-008`↓ — <что это решение значит, словами>
      - `<methodName>`↓ — <что это, словами>
    </Contract>

    <Rule id="FLOW_DIAGRAM_WHEN">
      When a decision is about a flow — data moving, state transitions, start / stop conditions — draw a tiny ASCII diagram; one glance beats a paragraph. This is the one place a fenced block is allowed inside the message (alignment). Keep it small. Example:

      ```
      [сеть] [auth] [конфиг]  ──всё есть──►  ОПРОС идёт
              ▲                                  │
              └──── снова есть ──── ПАУЗА ◄───────┘  что-то пропало
      ```
    </Rule>

    <Contract id="SIDE_DIVE_FORMAT">
      A sub-topic dive inside a step — two lines, not a summary. Live text:

      → ушли в <тема>
      ← <итог одной фразой>
    </Contract>

    <Contract id="HALT_FORMAT">
      Plain sentence first (what happened + what to do), token as a trailing tag. Live text:

      🛑 <что случилось по-человечески> — <что сделать оператору>   [H_*]
    </Contract>

    <Contract id="INFRASTRUCTURE_SPEC_STRUCTURE">
      Every top-level `## N.` section is wrapped with `<!--SECTION:UPPER_SNAKE-->…<!--/SECTION:UPPER_SNAKE-->` markers so any agent can pull a single section via `~/.claude/skills/sdd-execute/scripts/sdd extract <file> <NAME>`. Open marker IMMEDIATELY before the header; close marker IMMEDIATELY before the next same-level header (or EOF). Sub-sections (`### N.M`) and entity-level headers are NOT wrapped — they live inside the containing anchor. Section names are author-discretionary (the four templates below illustrate idiomatic choices); no name registry is enforced. Grammar source of truth: `extract-section.sh`. The four `SPEC_STRUCTURE` templates emit anchors by default — copy verbatim.

      ```markdown
      # <scope-name>: Infrastructure Specification

      <!--SECTION:SCOPE_TYPE-->
      ## scope-type
      infrastructure
      <!--/SECTION:SCOPE_TYPE-->

      <!--SECTION:VISION-->
      ## 1. Vision
      [Что включает dev/ops experience.]
      <!--/SECTION:VISION-->

      <!--SECTION:DIAGRAMS-->
      ## Diagrams
      [Per `SPEC_DIAGRAMS_SECTION_FORMAT` (visual-vocabulary). Минимум для infrastructure: поток dev-workflow. Каждая диаграмма в `<details>`, mermaid + подпись.]
      <!--/SECTION:DIAGRAMS-->

      <!--SECTION:TOOL_STACK-->
      ## 2. Tool Stack

      ### 2.1 Categories Covered
      [Список категорий + краткое обоснование исключений.]

      ### 2.2 Tool Choices
      | Category | Tool | Rationale |
      |---|---|---|
      | <category> | <tool> | D-NNN |
      <!--/SECTION:TOOL_STACK-->

      <!--SECTION:WORKFLOW_EXAMPLE-->
      ## 3. Developer Workflow Example
      [Shell-script: setup, daily flow, commit flow, debug flow.]
      <!--/SECTION:WORKFLOW_EXAMPLE-->

      <!--SECTION:FILE_STRUCTURE-->
      ## 4. File Structure
      [ASCII tree + File Mapping.]
      <!--/SECTION:FILE_STRUCTURE-->

      <!--SECTION:EFFECTIVE_RULES-->
      ## 5. Effective Rules (for cascade)
      | Rule | Category | Source |
      |---|---|---|
      | <rule-name> | coding | infra (D-NNN) |
      <!--/SECTION:EFFECTIVE_RULES-->

      <!--SECTION:VERIFICATION_COMMANDS-->
      ## 6. Verification Commands
      **Mandatory for infrastructure scopes.** After tool stack is finalized, record a table:
      | Command Name      | Invocation          |
      | typecheck-command | <actual invocation> |
      | test-command      | <actual invocation> |
      | lint-command      | <actual invocation> |
      | format-command    | <actual invocation> |
      | check-command     | <actual invocation> |

      Include only command names for tools present in the chosen stack.
      `check-command` is **always required** when the runtime setup rule (`nodejs-npm-setup` or equivalent) is active — it is the composed entry point that runs all active phases in `CheckPhaseOrder` order (typecheck → test → lint → format). Composition: chain invocations of each active phase command in that order. Task tickets use `check-command` as their single verification alias.
      <!--/SECTION:VERIFICATION_COMMANDS-->

      <!--SECTION:DECISION_LOG-->
      ## 7. Decision Log
      [D-NNN entries.]
      <!--/SECTION:DECISION_LOG-->

      <!--SECTION:SCOPE_DEPENDENCIES-->
      ## 8. Scope Dependencies
      - **Depends on:** None (infrastructure scopes are typically leaves)
      - **Provides rules to:** [list of product/library scopes that depend on this]
      <!--/SECTION:SCOPE_DEPENDENCIES-->

      <!--SECTION:BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS-->
      ## 9. Bootstrap Requirements
      | Requirement | Kind | Owner | Resolution |
      |---|---|---|---|
      <!-- Kind ∈ package | workspace-link | tool | file | external-type | env | service | structural -->
      <!-- Owner ∈ this-scope-task | external-prereq-scope | operator-action -->
      <!-- Empty list allowed only when STEP_7 audit produced zero external assumptions — declare it explicitly: "No external bootstrap required." -->
      <!--/SECTION:BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS-->

      <!--SECTION:HANDOFF-->
      ## 10. Handoff
      - **Setup tasks to scaffold:** [list]
      - **Effective rules ready for cascade:** see 5
      - **Verification Commands ready for cascade:** see 6
      - **Bootstrap tickets ready for cascade:** see 9
      - **Open risks:** [not-closed items]

      <!-- 10.1 Pivot Invalidation List — only in pivot mode -->
      <!--/SECTION:HANDOFF-->
      ```
    </Contract>

    <Contract id="CONTRACTS_SPEC_STRUCTURE">
      ```markdown
      # <scope-name>: Contracts Specification

      <!--SECTION:SCOPE_TYPE-->
      ## scope-type
      contracts
      <!--/SECTION:SCOPE_TYPE-->

      <!--SECTION:VISION-->
      ## 1. Vision
      [Что контракт определяет и для кого.]
      <!--/SECTION:VISION-->

      <!--SECTION:DIAGRAMS-->
      ## Diagrams
      [Per `SPEC_DIAGRAMS_SECTION_FORMAT` (visual-vocabulary). Минимум для contracts: ER схемы данных или sequence контракта. Каждая диаграмма в `<details>`, mermaid + подпись.]
      <!--/SECTION:DIAGRAMS-->

      <!--SECTION:INTERFACE_DECLARATION-->
      ## 2. Interface Declaration
      - **Schema format:** OpenAPI 3.x | gRPC proto3 | JSON Schema | GraphQL SDL
      - **Versioning scheme:** semver | date-based (YYYY-MM-DD) | none
      - **Namespace:** <api prefix / package name>
      - **Interfaces:** [список endpoint groups / message types / operations]
      <!--/SECTION:INTERFACE_DECLARATION-->

      <!--SECTION:VERSIONING_POLICY-->
      ## 3. Versioning Policy
      [Semver rules / breaking-change protocol / deprecation window.]
      <!--/SECTION:VERSIONING_POLICY-->

      <!--SECTION:COMPATIBILITY_MATRIX-->
      ## 4. Compatibility Matrix
      | Consumer scope | Min compatible version | Breaking change protocol |
      |---|---|---|
      <!--/SECTION:COMPATIBILITY_MATRIX-->

      <!--SECTION:DECISION_LOG-->
      ## 5. Decision Log
      [D-NNN entries.]
      <!--/SECTION:DECISION_LOG-->

      <!--SECTION:SCOPE_DEPENDENCIES-->
      ## 6. Scope Dependencies
      - **Depends on:** [infra scopes if any]
      - **Provides contracts to:** [product/library scopes]
      <!--/SECTION:SCOPE_DEPENDENCIES-->

      <!--SECTION:BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS-->
      ## 7. Bootstrap Requirements
      | Requirement | Kind | Owner | Resolution |
      |---|---|---|---|
      <!-- Empty list allowed only when STEP_7 audit produced zero external assumptions — declare explicitly. -->
      <!--/SECTION:BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS-->

      <!--SECTION:HANDOFF-->
      ## 8. Handoff
      - **Contract files to scaffold:** [schema files, codegen configs]
      - **Bootstrap tickets ready for cascade:** see 7
      - **Open risks:** [versioning gaps, unconfirmed consumers]
      <!--/SECTION:HANDOFF-->
      ```
    </Contract>

    <Contract id="LIBRARY_SPEC_STRUCTURE">
      ```markdown
      # <scope-name>: Library Specification

      <!--SECTION:SCOPE_TYPE-->
      ## scope-type
      library
      <!--/SECTION:SCOPE_TYPE-->

      <!--SECTION:VISION-->
      ## 1. Vision & Primary Goal
      [Что library делает; главная проблема, которую решает.]
      <!--/SECTION:VISION-->

      <!--SECTION:DIAGRAMS-->
      ## Diagrams
      [Per `SPEC_DIAGRAMS_SECTION_FORMAT` (visual-vocabulary). Минимум для library: архитектура (слои + направление зависимостей) + sequence главного вызова публичного API. Каждая диаграмма в `<details>`, mermaid + подпись.]
      <!--/SECTION:DIAGRAMS-->

      <!--SECTION:GOLDEN_DX-->
      ## 2. Approved Golden DX Example
      [Публичный API DX: init/setup + happy path + error path. Комментарии раскрывают намерение.]
      <!--/SECTION:GOLDEN_DX-->

      <!--SECTION:REQUIREMENTS_AND_CONSTRAINTS-->
      ## 3. Requirements & Constraints

      ### 3.1 Functional Requirements

      ### 3.2 Non-Functional Constraints

      ### 3.3 Out-of-Scope

      ### 3.4 Runtime Backing & Deferred Scope
      [Per `AX_RUNTIME_BACKING_EXPLICIT`. Posture per major capability + deferred parts.]

      ### 3.5 Rules
      | Rule | Category | Source |
      |---|---|---|
      <!--/SECTION:REQUIREMENTS_AND_CONSTRAINTS-->

      <!--SECTION:PUBLIC_API_SURFACE-->
      ## 4. Public API Surface
      [Ключевые exported interfaces, types, functions. Intent-level, без impl detail.]
      <!--/SECTION:PUBLIC_API_SURFACE-->

      <!--SECTION:ARCHITECTURE-->
      ## 5. Architecture
      [Выбранный design pattern. Кратко — rejected alternatives.]
      <!--/SECTION:ARCHITECTURE-->

      <!--SECTION:DECISION_LOG-->
      ## 6. Decision Log
      [D-NNN entries.]
      <!--/SECTION:DECISION_LOG-->

      <!--SECTION:SCOPE_DEPENDENCIES-->
      ## 7. Scope Dependencies
      - **Depends on:** [infra-*, contracts scopes]
      - **Provides to:** [consumer scopes]
      <!--/SECTION:SCOPE_DEPENDENCIES-->

      <!--SECTION:BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS-->
      ## 8. Bootstrap Requirements
      | Requirement | Kind | Owner | Resolution |
      |---|---|---|---|
      <!-- Empty list allowed only when STEP_7 audit produced zero external assumptions — declare explicitly. -->
      <!--/SECTION:BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS-->

      <!--SECTION:HANDOFF-->
      ## 9. Handoff to module-decomposition
      - **Areas requiring decomposition:** [list]
      - **Named abstractions:** [from Golden DX]
      - **Bootstrap tickets ready for cascade:** see 8
      - **Open risks:** [validation needs]

      <!-- 9.1 Pivot Invalidation List — only in pivot mode -->
      <!--/SECTION:HANDOFF-->
      ```
    </Contract>

    <Contract id="PRODUCT_SPEC_STRUCTURE">
      ```markdown
      # <scope-name>: Scope Specification

      <!--SECTION:SCOPE_TYPE-->
      ## scope-type
      product
      <!--/SECTION:SCOPE_TYPE-->

      <!--SECTION:VISION-->
      ## 1. Vision & Primary Goal
      <!--/SECTION:VISION-->

      <!--SECTION:DIAGRAMS-->
      ## Diagrams
      [Per `SPEC_DIAGRAMS_SECTION_FORMAT` (visual-vocabulary). Минимум для product: контекст (место скоупа среди соседей) + сценарии (кто и зачем приходит) + поток данных ключевого сценария. Каждая диаграмма в `<details>`, mermaid + подпись.]
      <!--/SECTION:DIAGRAMS-->

      <!--SECTION:PROJECT_TYPE-->
      ## 2. Project Type
      - **Type:** app[mobile|desktop|spa] | service-module-sdk | cli-utility
      - **Why this type:** [Краткое обоснование.]
      <!--/SECTION:PROJECT_TYPE-->

      <!--SECTION:GOLDEN_DX-->
      ## 3. Approved Golden DX Example
      [Commentary-rich: init/setup + happy path + error/degradation path.]
      <!--/SECTION:GOLDEN_DX-->

      <!--SECTION:REQUIREMENTS_AND_CONSTRAINTS-->
      ## 4. Requirements & Constraints

      ### 4.1 Functional Requirements

      ### 4.2 Non-Functional Constraints

      ### 4.3 Out-of-Scope

      ### 4.4 Runtime Backing & Deferred Scope
      [Per `AX_RUNTIME_BACKING_EXPLICIT`. Posture per major capability + deferred parts + trust boundaries требующие real runtime hook.]

      ### 4.5 Rules
      | Rule | Category | Source |
      |---|---|---|
      <!--/SECTION:REQUIREMENTS_AND_CONSTRAINTS-->

      <!--SECTION:ARCHITECTURE-->
      ## 5. High-Level Architecture
      [Выбранный вариант. Block diagram. Краткие описания.]

      ### 5.1 Rejected Alternatives
      <!--/SECTION:ARCHITECTURE-->

      <!--SECTION:DECISION_LOG-->
      ## 6. Decision Log
      [D-NNN entries.]
      <!--/SECTION:DECISION_LOG-->

      <!--SECTION:SCOPE_DEPENDENCIES-->
      ## 7. Scope Dependencies
      - **Depends on:** [infra-*, api-contracts, design-system-* scopes]
      - **Provides to:** [consumer scopes, if any]
      <!--/SECTION:SCOPE_DEPENDENCIES-->

      <!--SECTION:BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS-->
      ## 8. Bootstrap Requirements
      | Requirement | Kind | Owner | Resolution |
      |---|---|---|---|
      <!-- Empty list allowed only when STEP_7 audit produced zero external assumptions — declare explicitly. -->
      <!--/SECTION:BOOTSTRAP_REQUIREMENTS-->

      <!--SECTION:MODULE_MAP-->
      ## 9. Module Map
      [Appended by `module-decomposition`. Initially: «Modules not yet decomposed — run `module-decomposition <scope-name>`».]
      <!--/SECTION:MODULE_MAP-->

      <!--SECTION:HANDOFF-->
      ## 10. Handoff to module-decomposition
      - **Primary input:** `specs/<scope-name>/<scope-name>.spec.md`
      - **Areas requiring decomposition:** [list]
      - **Named abstractions:** [from Golden DX]
      - **Bootstrap tickets ready for cascade:** see 8
      - **Open risks:** [validation needs]

      <!-- 10.1 Pivot Invalidation List — only in pivot mode -->
      <!--/SECTION:HANDOFF-->
      ```
    </Contract>

    <Contract id="DECISION_LOG_ENTRY_FORMAT">
      No `Status` field (`AX_NO_DECORATIVE_FIELD`) — a decision is presumed current unless the title says otherwise; see `Supersedes` below for the one case that needs marking.

      ```markdown
      ### D-NNN — <short title>
      - **Recorded:** session Discovery, <scope-name>
      - **Why:** <operator's own wording>
      - **Risk accepted:** <if any>
      - **Rejected alternatives:** <list>
      - **Supersedes:** D-NNN (when superseding — the OLD entry's title gets a trailing `(superseded by D-NNN)`, not a Status field)
      ```
    </Contract>

    <Contract id="REWORK_DECISION_LOG_ENTRY_FORMAT">
      Used in `pivot` mode for each replaced decision. Self-contained: a future reader of the spec at this git ref must understand the rework without external context.

      ```markdown
      ### D-NNN — <short title> (rework)
      - **Recorded:** session Discovery, <scope-name>, pivot
      - **Supersedes:** D-MMM
      - **Pre-rework state:** git ref `<sha-or-tag>` (snapshot of spec immediately before this pivot)
      - **Was:** <one-sentence summary of the prior decision>
      - **Now:** <one-sentence summary of the replacement>
      - **Why:** <operator's own wording — what forced the rework>
      - **Risk accepted:** <if any>
      - **Downstream invalidation:** see Pivot Invalidation List
      ```

      Paired with Pivot Invalidation List entries that enumerate concrete downstream artifacts (`refine-module` targets, ticket reopens, rule revisits) for `audit` (`AX_STALE_AFTER_PIVOT_VERIFICATION`).
    </Contract>

    <Contract id="PIVOT_INVALIDATION_LIST_FORMAT">
      ```markdown
      ### 9.1 Pivot Invalidation List
      - **Module specs requiring refine:** [list with reasons]
      - **Tasks requiring reopen:** [TSK-NN — reason]
      - **Rules to revisit:** [list]
      ```
    </Contract>
  </OutputContracts>

  <ExamplePatterns>
    Discovery deliberately avoids one monolithic Golden Example — scope-spec shape varies too much across scope-types. Below are intentionally compact, freeform sketches; treat them as orientation, not templates. The normative shapes live in the SPEC_STRUCTURE contracts.

    <Pattern id="EXAMPLE_INTENT_STOP">
      **FALLBACK SHAPE — AskUserQuestion tool unavailable.** With the tool available, each of the two развилки below goes through interview-protocol's `QUESTION_MESSAGE_FORMAT` — one per message, with its own ASK. This pattern shows the STEP_2 (intent) boundary layout itself: breadcrumb, understanding block at the top, questions, footnotes. Live message text, NOT a code block.

      DSC_R2 · backend · greenfield · conf=med
      Намерение ▶️ · Требования ░ · Golden DX ░ · Архитектура ░ · Спека ░

      ## 🦾 backend — IMAP-сервис: тянет письма и отдаёт потребителю

      ### 🤔 Как я понял задачу
      > _Можно не читать, если доверяешь._
      > Сервис без UI: держит длинное IMAP-соединение, ловит новые письма и пересылает потребителю. Своего хранилища писем нет — только пересылка.
      > **Где могу ошибаться:** считаю, что при обрыве связи мы продолжаем с места по `UIDVALIDITY`↓, а не читаем заново — это вопрос Q2.

      ### ❓ Вопросы — реши 2
      «годится» = беру рекомендации, или поправь по номерам.

      ❓ **Q1 · Сколько аккаунтов на один запуск?**
      ✅ **Один аккаунт** — рекомендую: проще, покрывает v1.
      Иначе:
      - **Несколько** — _за: гибче · против: нужен пул соединений и изоляция, дороже к v1_

      ❓ **Q2 · Что делаем при обрыве связи?**
      ✅ **Продолжаем с места** по `UIDVALIDITY`↓ — рекомендую: не теряем письма.
      Иначе:
      - **Читаем с нуля** — _за: проще · против: дубли и потери на больших ящиках_

      ### 📎 Что значат ссылки
      - `UIDVALIDITY`↓ — метка IMAP-папки; по ней понятно, можно ли продолжить с сохранённого места или папку пересобрали
    </Pattern>

    <Pattern id="EXAMPLE_INFRA_STACK_STOP">
      A STEP_3+4 boundary for infrastructure: the whole stack at once instead of one negotiation per category. Live message text; the table renders as a real table.

      DSC_R4 · infra-base · greenfield · conf=high
      Намерение ✅ · Стек ▶️ · Workflow ░ · Спека ░

      ## 🏗️ infra-base — минимальный TS-стек

      ### Предлагаю весь набор сразу
      | категория | беру | почему | замена |
      |---|---|---|---|
      | пакеты | pnpm | быстрый, честный lock | npm · yarn |
      | типы | tsc | без сборщика на старте | — |
      | линт+формат | biome | один вместо двух | eslint+prettier |
      | тесты | vitest | родной для vite | jest |
      | git-хуки | lefthook | быстрый, без зависимостей | husky |

      🙋 Правь любую строку или скажи «годится» — остальное беру как есть.
    </Pattern>

    <Pattern id="EXAMPLE_INFRASTRUCTURE_SPEC">
      ```markdown
      # infra-base: Infrastructure Specification

      ## 1. Vision
      Минимальный TS-стек: быстрый CI, deterministic install, zero-config formatter.

      ## 2. Tool Stack
      | Category | Tool | Rationale |
      |---|---|---|
      | package-management | pnpm | D-001 (lockfile determinism vs npm) |
      | type-check | tsc | D-002 (no transpiler — esbuild на bundler-этапе) |
      | linting+formatting | biome | D-003 (один tool вместо eslint+prettier) |

      ## 3. Dev Workflow Example
      ```bash
      pnpm install
      pnpm typecheck && pnpm test && pnpm lint
      ```

      ## 5. Effective Rules (cascade)
      | Rule | Category | File | Source |
      |---|---|---|---|
      | <rule-id> | coding | ai/directives/coding/<rule>.xml | infra (D-NNN) |
      | <rule-id> | infra | ai/directives/infra/<rule>.xml | infra (D-NNN) |
      ```
    </Pattern>

    <Pattern id="EXAMPLE_CONTRACTS_SPEC">
      ```markdown
      # api-contracts: Contracts Specification

      ## 2. Interface Declaration
      - Schema format: OpenAPI 3.1
      - Versioning: date-based (`/v2026-05-01/...`)
      - Namespace: `api.example.com`

      ## 3. Versioning Policy
      Breaking changes требуют новой date-версии + 6 мес deprecation окна; non-breaking — same version, additive only.

      ## 4. Compatibility Matrix
      | Consumer | Min version | Breaking protocol |
      |---|---|---|
      | backend | 2026-05-01 | dual-publish on transition |
      | web | 2026-05-01 | client codegen via openapi-typescript |
      ```
    </Pattern>

    <Pattern id="EXAMPLE_LIBRARY_SPEC">
      ```markdown
      # logger-core: Library Specification

      ## 2. Approved Golden DX Example
      ```ts
      // happy path
      const log = createLogger({ name: 'payments', level: 'info' })
      log.info({ orderId }, 'charge succeeded')

      // degradation: transport unavailable
      log.transport.on('error', (err) => log.warn({ err }, 'fallback to stdout'))
      ```

      ## 4. Public API Surface
      - `createLogger(config) → Logger`
      - `Logger.{ trace, debug, info, warn, error, fatal }(payload, message)`
      - `Logger.child(bindings) → Logger`
      ```
    </Pattern>

    <Pattern id="EXAMPLE_PRODUCT_SPEC">
      ```markdown
      # backend: Scope Specification

      ## 2. Project Type
      - Type: service-module-sdk
      - Why: IMAP backend, без UI; transport — long-lived TCP + outbound HTTP.

      ## 3. Approved Golden DX Example (UX-flow)
      Сценарий «новое письмо»: IMAP IDLE keep-alive ловит EXISTS → forward to consumer → ack;
      degradation: server reset → exponential backoff + restore по UIDVALIDITY.

      ## 4.4 Runtime Backing & Deferred Scope
      - IMAP IDLE: `real-runtime` (production-critical).
      - OAuth refresh: `real-runtime`.
      - Webhook dispatcher: `simulation` в v1 (deferred → TSK-stub до реальной integration).
      ```
    </Pattern>
  </ExamplePatterns>
</SddDiscovery>